@using KnockBox.Core.Components.Shared
@using KnockBox.DndMapper.Services.State.Games
@using KnockBox.DndMapper.Services.State.Games.Data
@inherits DisposableComponent

@*  Markup overlay (v1.x, §5.6).

    Two render branches sit between the image layer and the token layer of
    MapCanvas:

    Host + toggle on  → interactive SvgDrawingCanvas with full toolbar.
    Everyone else     → read-only canvas with ShowToolbar=false; the
                        component's pointer-events:none lets player drags
                        fall through to the token layer beneath. *@

@*  Two-mode markup surface — see §5.6 of the GDD.
    The wrapper is positioned absolutely over the map by MapCanvas's CSS
    (`.dndm-markup-overlay-mount`). We size the inner SvgDrawingCanvas at
    100% / 100% so it fills that mount; both surfaces use the same
    viewBox in grid-cell units, so strokes land on the same map cells the
    host clicked regardless of container aspect ratio. *@
@*  The drawing canvas runs in *pixel space* — viewBox is sized in CSS
    pixels (cells × CellPixels) so a 1-pixel stroke on screen is a
    1-pixel stroke in the saved markup. On commit we wrap the result in
    a `scale(1/CellPixels)` transform so the persisted markup is in the
    map's cell coordinate system and renders directly inside MapCanvas's
    cell-unit viewBox.

    BackgroundSvgContent is shown as a reference layer behind the active
    drawing; it's already in cell coords (post-conversion), so we wrap
    it in the inverse scale to compose it with the pixel-space canvas. *@
@{
    var pxW = Map.Grid.WidthCells * Map.Grid.CellPixels;
    var pxH = Map.Grid.HeightCells * Map.Grid.CellPixels;
    var bg = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Map.MarkupSvg)
        ? null
        : $"<g transform=\"scale({Map.Grid.CellPixels})\">{Map.MarkupSvg}</g>";
}

@if (IsActive)
{
    <SvgDrawingCanvas @ref="_canvas"
                      Width="100%"
                      Height="100%"
                      ShowToolbar="true"
                      BackgroundColor="transparent"
                      ViewBoxWidth="@pxW"
                      ViewBoxHeight="@pxH"
                      BackgroundSvgContent="@bg"
                      StrokeCompleted="OnStrokeCompleted" />
}
else
{
    <SvgDrawingCanvas Width="100%"
                      Height="100%"
                      ShowToolbar="false"
                      BackgroundColor="transparent"
                      ViewBoxWidth="@pxW"
                      ViewBoxHeight="@pxH"
                      BackgroundSvgContent="@bg" />
}
